course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 23
title: Animal sounds, rain, snow, sun and rainbows
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Animals "speak" differently in every language: a Ukrainian cat says "myAu", a dog says "hav-hav", and a rooster says "ku-ku-ri-ku".
explain: Ukrainian rain doesn't fall, it goes: "ide doshch" is "rain is going", it's raining, and "ide snih" is it's snowing — and Sashko's mum will answer "bery parasolku", take an umbrella.
explain: Watch two words that sound almost alike: "kholodno" (it's cold, with a rough kh) and "hoLOdnyi" (hungry, with a soft h).
explain: After the rain comes the veselka, the rainbow — from veselyi, cheerful, so it's "the merry one" — and kalyuzhi, puddles, which every kid jumps in while mum shouts "ne liz u kalyuzhu!".
fact: A Ukrainian cat says "myAu", a dog says "hav-hav", and a rooster says "ku-ku-ri-ku".
fact: "pohOda" means weather; "yaka pohOda?" means "what's the weather like?", and "nepohoda" means bad weather.
fact: "sOnyachno" means sunny; "sontse" means the sun.
fact: "ide doshch" means "it's raining", literally "rain is going", and the usual reply is "bery parasolku", take an umbrella.
fact: "ide snih" means "it's snowing", literally "snow is going", and kids answer "urA!".
fact: "kholodno" means "it's cold"; a mum may say "odyahnysya teplishe", dress warmer.
fact: "kholodno" (cold, with kh) and "hoLOdnyi" (hungry, with h) sound alike but mean different things.
fact: "zharko" means "it's hot", and "tEplo" means "it's warm".
fact: "vItEr" means wind, and "sylnyi vItEr" means a strong wind.
fact: "hrIm" means thunder and "blyskavka" means lightning; in a storm the safe place is indoors.
fact: "veselka" means rainbow; the word is linked to veselyi, cheerful.
fact: "kalyuzha" means puddle, and "ne liz u kalyuzhu!" means "don't go in the puddle!".
exercise: What does a Ukrainian dog say? | hav-hav | woof-woof | myAu | ku-ku-ri-ku
exercise: What does a Ukrainian cat say? | myAu | hav-hav | khru-khru | ku-ku-ri-ku
exercise: Sashko asks "yaka pohOda?". What does he want to know? | What the weather is like | What time it is | What's for dinner | What day it is
exercise: Sashko looks out: "sOnyachno!". What's it like outside? | Sunny | Rainy, wet | Snowy, white | Windy, gusty
exercise: Sashko's mum says "ide doshch". What is happening? | It's raining | Someone is coming | It's snowing | The bus is going
exercise: Sashko's mum says "ide doshch". What does she add? | bery parasolku | ne bIisya | pomyi ruky | DObroi NOchi
exercise: Sashko shouts "ide snih!". What's happening? | It's snowing | It's raining | It's sunny | It's hailing
exercise: It's minus five outside and Sashko's mum says "kholodno". What does she mean? | It's cold | It's hungry | It's hot | It's windy
exercise: Sashko says "zharko" in July at the beach. What does he mean? | It's hot | It's cold | It's raining | It's late
exercise: A nice spring day, not too hot. Which word fits? | tEplo | zharko | kholodno | vItEr
exercise: Trees bend and hats fly off. Which word do you hear? | vItEr | veselka | snih | sOnyachno
exercise: What are "hrIm" and "blyskavka"? | Thunder and lightning | Rain and snow together | Wind and clouds | Sun and rainbow
exercise: After the rain, Sashko points up: "veselka!". What does he see? | A rainbow | A rain cloud | A plane up high | A shooting star
exercise: Sashko's mum yells "ne liz u kalyuzhu!". What should Sashko stay out of? | The puddle | The garden pond | The muddy field | The hot bath
test: my mate says "kholodno" and "hoLOdnyi" are the same word. right? | No, kholodno is cold and hoLOdnyi is hungry | Yes, both mean cold | Yes, both mean hungry | No, both mean hot
test: Why does Ukrainian say rain is "going" instead of falling? | That's just how the language says it | Because rain walks along the ground | Because "ide" means "falls" in old Ukrainian | Because it's a joke word
test: In a cartoon a Ukrainian dog barks "woof-woof". What would a Ukrainian kid expect? | hav-hav | myAu | ku-ku-ri-ku | woof-woof is the same in every language
test: bloody storm, hrIm and blyskavka, and your mate wants to stay on the football pitch. Best move? | Go indoors | Stand under a tall tree | Keep playing, it's fine | Lie on the pitch
test: Why is a rainbow called "veselka"? | It is linked to veselyi, cheerful | It means "seven colours" | It means "bridge in the sky" | It comes from a word for rain
test: Sashko's mum says "odyahnysya teplishe" when you leave. What's the weather? | Cold | Hot | Sunny and warm | No weather at all
test: It's thirty degrees and Sashko's granny still says "tEplo". Which word would fit better? | zharko | kholodno | snih | vItEr
test: Your little sister jumps into a big puddle in new shoes. What does Sashko's mum shout? | ne liz u kalyuzhu! | ide snih! | sOnyachno! | yaka pohOda?
test: "ide snih" and Sashko shouts "urA!". Why is he happy? | Snow means snowballs and fun | He hates the snow and the cold | School is starting | It's time for bed
test: Grey sky, cold drizzle all day. What would Sashko's mum call it? | nepohoda | veselka | sOnyachno | zharko
test: What would happen if you said "ya kholodno" to Sashko's mum hoping for food? | She'd think you were talking about the cold, not hunger | She'd bring dinner at once | She'd say "na zdorOv'ya" | She'd laugh and give sweets
exam: Determine the meaning of the expression "ide doshch" when rendered literally and in its customary sense. | Literally "rain is going", meaning "it is raining" | Literally "rain has gone", meaning "the rain has ceased" | Literally "go to the rain", meaning "go outside" | Literally "rain is coming", meaning "rain is forecast for tomorrow"
exam: It is given that the adverb "kholodno" and the adjective "hoLOdnyi" are phonetically similar. Select the statement most consistent with the established facts concerning their meanings. | The former signifies coldness and the latter hunger | Both signify coldness | Both signify hunger | The former signifies hunger and the latter coldness
exam: Identify the sound conventionally attributed to a dog in the Ukrainian language. | hav-hav | myAu | ku-ku-ri-ku | woof-woof
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the noun "veselka"? | It denotes a rainbow and is related to a word signifying cheerfulness | It denotes a puddle and is related to a word signifying wetness | It denotes thunder and is related to a word signifying noise | It denotes the wind and is related to a word signifying speed
